" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/problem "Given a cone, find the differential $\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the cone is $V = \dfrac{1}{3} \pi r^2h.$ Let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\frac{∂V}{∂r})\,\text{d}r+(\frac{∂V}{∂h})\,\text{d}h \\  & =\frac{2}{3}\pi rh\,\text{d}r+\frac{1}{3}\pi {r}^{2}\,\text{d}h.\end{aligned}
$$

For $r=6$ and $h=3,$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(6,3) & =\frac{2}{3}\pi \cdot 6\cdot 3\,\text{d}r+\frac{1}{3}\pi \cdot {6}^{2}\,\text{d}h \\  & =12\pi \,\text{d}r+12\pi \,\text{d}h.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186125053 :question/id #uuid "f9652424-c2ee-5706-a3c2-84b62902c6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37295"} {:db/id 17592186125057 :question/id #uuid "35be243b-50e1-5aab-aebe-0bcb17e84ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-37308"} {:db/id 17592186284605 :question/id #uuid "56bdc966-10cb-53e1-a679-ccd338b6c565" :question/math-academy-id "q-168150" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ff9de6-82b9-57fd-8352-549a13be4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\,\text{d}a+9\,\text{d}b+64\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186282128} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0312d-ecd7-57dc-9d88-15ff8ec29a6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\,\text{d}a+24\,\text{d}b+48\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186282330} {:answer/id #uuid "55b45edc-b405-5927-afc4-f43852758102" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\,\text{d}a+48\,\text{d}b+18\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186282415} {:answer/id #uuid "8abb4554-2c3b-5729-8dbb-6cd315e8c4a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\,\text{d}a+3\,\text{d}b+9\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186282885} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a31cef-0e81-5be3-8ef5-5772fcc10f4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\,\text{d}a+24\,\text{d}b+9\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186283675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b45edc-b405-5927-afc4-f43852758102" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\,\text{d}a+48\,\text{d}b+18\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186282415} :answer-field/id #uuid "8563a43e-521b-5142-9d50-25857d86ed4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=8.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\frac{∂V}{∂a})\,\text{d}a+(\frac{∂V}{∂b})\,\text{d}b+(\frac{∂V}{∂c})\,\text{d}c \\  & =bc\,\text{d}a+ac\,\text{d}b+ab\,\text{d}c\end{aligned}
$$

For $a=6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=8,$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(6,3,8) & =3\cdot 8\,\text{d}a+6\cdot 8\,\text{d}b+6\cdot 3\,\text{d}c \\  & =24\,\text{d}a+48\,\text{d}b+18\,\text{d}c.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186348270 :question/id #uuid "eb226681-0351-549f-b81b-c5875e8a6018" :question/math-academy-id "q-79746" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393a2da4-2037-5f88-af20-25566e93ee10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\,\text{d}a+10\,\text{d}b+2\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186345839} {:answer/id #uuid "5f766058-5345-504f-969f-c6f41943d337" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\,\text{d}a+5\,\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186346176} {:answer/id #uuid "8c626268-e771-514e-9e16-2f37c28e6a23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\,\text{d}a+5\,\text{d}b+4\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186346570} {:answer/id #uuid "bd042560-3b82-573a-bb03-933f8a2d73a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\,\text{d}a+2\,\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186346994} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe3e38f-3a94-535d-a8d4-214ff917e54f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\,\text{d}a+25\,\text{d}b+4\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186347278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd042560-3b82-573a-bb03-933f8a2d73a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\,\text{d}a+2\,\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}c" :db/id 17592186346994} :answer-field/id #uuid "001e58e4-393c-54c0-893b-8d317963f75b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\frac{∂V}{∂a})\,\text{d}a+(\frac{∂V}{∂b})\,\text{d}b+(\frac{∂V}{∂c})\,\text{d}c \\  & =bc\,\text{d}a+ac\,\text{d}b+ab\,\text{d}c\end{aligned}
$$

For $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1,$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(2,5,1) & =5\cdot 1\,\text{d}a+2\cdot 1\,\text{d}b+5\cdot 2\,\text{d}c \\  & =5\,\text{d}a+2\,\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}c.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186348271 :question/id #uuid "6f0e792d-8d7d-5fba-9784-ec8ac9d90e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-79747"} {:db/id 17592186348321 :question/id #uuid "00fbdc66-0e7c-5f09-b062-4832cba6efd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79958" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16681232-d875-51d4-8775-995a4bdc038d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\,\text{d}r+4\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186345550} {:answer/id #uuid "19759a8b-a542-5b9d-ac6b-52141e753e0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{2}{5}\,\text{d}r+\frac{3}{10}\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186345575} {:answer/id #uuid "39123486-345d-5915-be9e-6db3c7d3e8ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{3}{5}\,\text{d}r+\frac{4}{5}\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186345838} {:answer/id #uuid "65039564-9812-5d42-8c1d-dcf7c26c5410" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{3}{10}\,\text{d}r+\frac{2}{5}\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186346227} {:answer/id #uuid "d3029df8-972e-585a-af9f-01f6b385376c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{4}{5}\,\text{d}r+\frac{3}{5}\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186347196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39123486-345d-5915-be9e-6db3c7d3e8ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\frac{3}{5}\,\text{d}r+\frac{4}{5}\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186345838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7bb7a4f-725e-5075-b3cf-da3a1d65ad3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\text{d}l$ for the slant height $l$ of the right cone if its base radius is $r=3$ and height $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The slant height of the right cone is $l=\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}.$ Let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}l(r,h) & =(\frac{∂l}{∂r})\,\text{d}r+(\frac{∂l}{∂h})\,\text{d}h \\  & =\frac{r}{\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\,\text{d}r+\frac{h}{\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\,\text{d}h\end{aligned}
$$

For $r=3$  and $h=4$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(3,4) & =\frac{3}{\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\,\text{d}r+\frac{4}{\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\,\text{d}h \\  & =\frac{3}{5}\,\text{d}r+\frac{4}{5}\,\text{d}h.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186372349 :question/id #uuid "8ad2cde7-ba79-5b5e-a983-25dd72f62433" :question/math-academy-id "q-168149" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b745db-632d-5d42-b0cb-5988bdfee9f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\,\text{d}a+10\text{d}b+12\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372351} {:answer/id #uuid "883260a4-04ea-5104-a33b-20e8f9ddd2f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\,\text{d}a+15\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372352} {:answer/id #uuid "643fcc04-a886-51b8-b744-bc0e4ca00b64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\,\text{d}a+8\text{d}b+10\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372353} {:answer/id #uuid "71ae431e-e7e1-5107-b656-de08bcff04a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\,\text{d}a+20\text{d}b+24\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372354} {:answer/id #uuid "84c9935a-e18a-5e11-96e5-9d9b09b670a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\,\text{d}a+4\text{d}b+12\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0b745db-632d-5d42-b0cb-5988bdfee9f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\,\text{d}a+10\text{d}b+12\,\text{d}h" :db/id 17592186372351} :answer-field/id #uuid "22fd4b2f-5a66-564b-904f-0e3de82edda3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186372350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Given a right triangular prism, find the differential $\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ if the height is $h=5$ and the base is a right triangle with legs $a=4$ and $b=6.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a given right triangular prism is $V=\frac{abh}{2}.$ Let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\frac{∂V}{∂a})\,\text{d}a+(\frac{∂V}{∂b})\,\text{d}b+(\frac{∂V}{∂h})\,\text{d}h \\  & =\frac{bh}{2}\,\text{d}a+\frac{ah}{2}\,\text{d}b+\frac{ab}{2}\,\text{d}h\end{aligned}
$$

For $a=4,$ $b=6,$ and $h=5,$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}V(4,6,5) & =\frac{6\cdot 5}{2}\,\text{d}a+\frac{4\cdot 5}{2}\,\text{d}b+\frac{4\cdot 6}{2}\,\text{d}h \\  & =15\,\text{d}a+10\text{d}b+12\,\text{d}h.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186375377 :question/id #uuid "b1c3c795-7c5d-549b-81c7-5bd23b4b11f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79744"} {:db/id 17592186375384 :question/id #uuid "ddfbfeb1-47d1-5e4a-929c-ad72e52c8b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79959"} {:db/id 17592186393968 :question/id #uuid "385e16b4-f8da-593a-b039-318c70552c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79745"} {:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124376 :question/id #uuid "0c4816ed-fba0-5dc7-94ba-646b875d653d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6875"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186125055 :question/id #uuid "a5a0b5eb-1d35-5e16-8d84-c218ac09143a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37306"} {:db/id 17592186125226 :question/id #uuid "b3d2ae44-10d9-5db3-858c-8da3c9096267" :question/math-academy-id "q-79814"} {:db/id 17592186284577 :question/id #uuid "7d3c9249-0c86-56ad-b2f3-cb8e23eff378" :question/math-academy-id "q-167166"} {:db/id 17592186284578 :question/id #uuid "7ec3ee1a-9141-5963-a032-74283a58c8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-167168"} {:db/id 17592186348287 :question/id #uuid "660c5793-fb22-5668-8662-5f26917785f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79809"} {:db/id 17592186348289 :question/id #uuid "b3a7a619-a349-551d-ae22-16b086347f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79810"} {:db/id 17592186348290 :question/id #uuid "b4eb830a-39de-5829-9892-2282b1954159" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811"} {:db/id 17592186348291 :question/id #uuid "a59577cc-4986-5576-9592-2ad42c96ff6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79812"} {:db/id 17592186348292 :question/id #uuid "01cbcb4d-5667-5e3e-806c-ef99a5773f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813"} {:db/id 17592186348293 :question/id #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79815"} {:db/id 17592186354501 :question/id #uuid "d42b8603-2969-573d-b945-d7e1061c7be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86715"} {:db/id 17592186354502 :question/id #uuid "ed7f3fdb-ae1d-565d-b08f-f4ca7b1197c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86716"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"} {:db/id 17592186124376 :question/id #uuid "0c4816ed-fba0-5dc7-94ba-646b875d653d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6875" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/problem "If $f (x, y) = xe^y ,$ use the differential $\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & ={e}^{y}\,\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(5,0),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(5,0) & ={e}^{0}\,\text{d}x+5{e}^{0}\,\text{d}y \\  & =1\,\text{d}x+5\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(5 + \Delta x, 0 + \Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
\Delta f \approx 1 \, \Delta x + 5 \, \Delta y.
$$

Moving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-5=-3, \\ Δy & =1-0=1.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\,Δx+5\,Δy \\  & ≈1\cdot (-3)+5\cdot (1) \\  & ≈2.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2.$"} {:db/id 17592186125055 :question/id #uuid "a5a0b5eb-1d35-5e16-8d84-c218ac09143a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37306" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81763c7d-e9ed-5589-9527-e4f237b7320d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf \approx 0.1" :db/id 17592186122931} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8999ef-680a-545e-ac09-71dfcdd3b63b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf \approx 0.3" :db/id 17592186122932} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea0548-b7c1-5bad-97f0-772df63e1d8f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf \approx - 0.5" :db/id 17592186122933} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f7c64a-223c-5aea-a503-0bceb1da88f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf \approx 0.5" :db/id 17592186122934} {:answer/id #uuid "73f4c11e-3050-54b8-9c76-cfb8aa6ab07d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf \approx - 0.3" :db/id 17592186122935}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca377d22-89aa-51a0-900c-a45e47680e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b0dfa1-a3bd-5d86-841e-767261c7fdb4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈0.1" :db/id 17592186373014} {:answer/id #uuid "b518876a-d6a2-57b2-92cf-9d904e6dd10f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈-0.5" :db/id 17592186373015} {:answer/id #uuid "6c65d1de-3197-52f8-9f96-cfeb62d0ce2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈0.3" :db/id 17592186373016} {:answer/id #uuid "6123d165-543d-5503-b3d6-b4c4dfd85d81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈-0.3" :db/id 17592186373017} {:answer/id #uuid "98e43f2c-7032-55d3-ac19-6318a10d07a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈0.5" :db/id 17592186373018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98e43f2c-7032-55d3-ac19-6318a10d07a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Δf≈0.5" :db/id 17592186373018} :answer-field/id #uuid "c754f0c6-e9f0-5f7b-a4f5-f0c361b3d140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y,$ use the differential $\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(4\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =-\operatorname{sin}⁡x\,\text{d}x+1\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(4\pi ,8),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(4\pi ,8) & =-\operatorname{sin}⁡4\pi \,\text{d}x+1\,\text{d}y \\  & =0\,\text{d}x+1\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(4\pi +Δx,8+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈0\,Δx+1\,Δy.
$$

Moving from $(4\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =13-4\pi , \\ Δy & =8.5-8=0.5.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\,Δx+1\,Δy \\  & ≈0\cdot (13-4\pi )+1\cdot (0.5) \\  & ≈0.5.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$"} {:db/id 17592186125226 :question/id #uuid "b3d2ae44-10d9-5db3-858c-8da3c9096267" :question/math-academy-id "q-79814"} {:db/id 17592186284577 :question/id #uuid "7d3c9249-0c86-56ad-b2f3-cb8e23eff378" :question/math-academy-id "q-167166"} {:db/id 17592186284578 :question/id #uuid "7ec3ee1a-9141-5963-a032-74283a58c8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-167168" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6165f20f-a911-5f52-b154-c9b7146be783" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186282518} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c165-90a1-52b3-950c-0524d0a78d44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186282758} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186283452} {:answer/id #uuid "d1675874-99c7-53bb-a75e-8153ff7cfa34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186283517} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5c20cb-3aaf-53d0-987f-6298d27bfd52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186283756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186283452} :answer-field/id #uuid "8707a5fd-f08d-52ad-bc81-12c73efa5a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz),$ use the differential $\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y+(\frac{∂f}{∂z})\,\text{d}z \\  & =\frac{1}{x}\,\text{d}x+\frac{1}{y}\,\text{d}y+\frac{1}{z}\,\text{d}z\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\,\text{d}x+1\,\text{d}y+1\,\text{d}z.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,1+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈Δx+Δy+Δz.
$$

Moving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\ Δy & =3-1=2 \\ Δz & =4-1=3.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈Δx+Δy+Δz \\  & ≈1+2+3 \\  & ≈6.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$"} {:db/id 17592186348287 :question/id #uuid "660c5793-fb22-5668-8662-5f26917785f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79809" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bf48df2-dc34-52ca-93a9-b4daed0d2e97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1" :db/id 17592186346435} {:answer/id #uuid "8429f3a8-0088-5774-8b2b-6ec526d65653" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186346501} {:answer/id #uuid "92ce89f6-9755-5e6f-839c-c0fe25166097" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id 17592186346635} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7c1da-a44a-587a-bb11-6f4446367efa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id 17592186346798} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c5f6c8-cebe-5787-bdab-e969594c0fc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186347231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7c1da-a44a-587a-bb11-6f4446367efa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id 17592186346798} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e64129a-5def-586b-ba54-9ee7a632757d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,\frac{\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =\operatorname{sin}⁡y\,\text{d}x+x\operatorname{cos}⁡y\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(2,\frac{\pi }{2}),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(2,\frac{\pi }{2}) & =\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{2})\,\text{d}x+2\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{\pi }{2})\,\text{d}y \\  & =1\,\text{d}x+0\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,\frac{\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈1\,Δx+0\,Δy.
$$

Moving from $(2,\frac{\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\ Δy & =1.5-\frac{\pi }{2}.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\,Δx+0\,Δy \\  & ≈1\cdot (-0.5)+0\cdot (1.5-\frac{\pi }{2}) \\  & ≈-0.5.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$"} {:db/id 17592186348289 :question/id #uuid "b3a7a619-a349-551d-ae22-16b086347f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79810" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434f2e6c-b51e-56e2-b3b6-bfea95031930" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id 17592186345933} {:answer/id #uuid "4357d5f4-8f26-5f42-b673-2b35c8637752" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id 17592186345936} {:answer/id #uuid "45342e19-03d6-5c5e-82c8-aa1f3a9c2881" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id 17592186345952} {:answer/id #uuid "6975986f-f6e6-55f7-9e5c-13e49e2e5623" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id 17592186346267} {:answer/id #uuid "d185e4fb-7b96-564b-a235-1e5d04c537b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id 17592186347188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45342e19-03d6-5c5e-82c8-aa1f3a9c2881" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id 17592186345952} :answer-field/id #uuid "922aa6d1-ce19-5aa5-aed9-a049d29daf39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =2xy\,\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{y}})\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(0,4),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\cdot 0\cdot 4\,\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{4}})\,\text{d}y \\  & =0\,\text{d}x+\frac{1}{4}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈0\,Δx+\frac{1}{4}\,Δy.
$$

Moving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\ Δy & =5-4=1.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\,Δx+\frac{1}{4}\,Δy \\  & ≈0\cdot (-1)+\frac{1}{4}\cdot (1) \\  & ≈0.25.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$"} {:db/id 17592186348290 :question/id #uuid "b4eb830a-39de-5829-9892-2282b1954159" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811"} {:db/id 17592186348291 :question/id #uuid "a59577cc-4986-5576-9592-2ad42c96ff6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79812"} {:db/id 17592186348292 :question/id #uuid "01cbcb4d-5667-5e3e-806c-ef99a5773f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813"} {:db/id 17592186348293 :question/id #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79815"} {:db/id 17592186354501 :question/id #uuid "d42b8603-2969-573d-b945-d7e1061c7be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86715" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e7b4730-07bc-55e3-a6a1-1f8f741572c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186352147} {:answer/id #uuid "82af2898-1b6c-5362-bd25-0fa6ec45995a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186352706} {:answer/id #uuid "a1fd631b-991b-54d5-9cc6-8f3cbf84de69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186352953} {:answer/id #uuid "b1408a5a-b1a7-5eba-8e9e-add29f5b3b8f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186353083} {:answer/id #uuid "bd42095a-e62b-516a-8666-20268ea4ab47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186353179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7b4730-07bc-55e3-a6a1-1f8f741572c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186352147} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb9582cd-856c-58b5-9f89-f922a1fb2623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{3}-y),$ use the differential $\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y+(\frac{∂f}{∂z})\,\text{d}z \\  & =2x\,\text{d}x-(\frac{4}{{z}^{3}-y})\,\text{d}y+(\frac{12{z}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-y})\,\text{d}z\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(2,-3,1),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(2,-3,1) & =(2\cdot 2)\,\text{d}x-(\frac{4}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\,\text{d}y+(\frac{12\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\,\text{d}z \\  & =4\,\text{d}x-\,\text{d}y+3\,\text{d}z.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,-3+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈4\,Δx-\,Δy+3\,Δz.
$$

Moving from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\ Δy & =-2-(-3)=1 \\ Δz & =2-1=1.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\,Δx-1\,Δy+3\,Δz \\  & ≈4\cdot (-1)-1\cdot 1+3\cdot 1 \\  & ≈-2.\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-2.$"} {:db/id 17592186354502 :question/id #uuid "ed7f3fdb-ae1d-565d-b08f-f4ca7b1197c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86716"} {:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124377 :question/id #uuid "febc72d4-b1b8-52c9-a805-013b30695077" :question/math-academy-id "e-6876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186125051 :question/id #uuid "c4f3d8c4-d372-512f-a12b-325fd0cb0744" :question/math-academy-id "q-37281"} {:db/id 17592186125227 :question/id #uuid "d809b121-2117-5794-bb89-efcea5057754" :question/math-academy-id "q-79837"} {:db/id 17592186284580 :question/id #uuid "862d8f4a-ef1a-5220-a521-4d8cdae9c576" :question/math-academy-id "q-167170"} {:db/id 17592186284581 :question/id #uuid "9eb149a3-bdcc-552e-a682-ced670cd4013" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171"} {:db/id 17592186348300 :question/id #uuid "2e921641-2f51-5af1-85b0-a27e011ac9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79836"} {:db/id 17592186348301 :question/id #uuid "3c9a3a87-a14e-5269-82cd-f0a320dbf13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79839"} {:db/id 17592186348302 :question/id #uuid "54765bb6-f6e1-50b8-8581-fc92ecfe7a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-79840"} {:db/id 17592186348303 :question/id #uuid "6467df78-2b9b-5255-9ede-8186a25e3f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79841"} {:db/id 17592186348304 :question/id #uuid "1639694c-cbca-54ad-bf88-2314edcecfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79842"} {:db/id 17592186348306 :question/id #uuid "6749d91f-49a8-5f4a-a9d1-89ed10459b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79876"} {:db/id 17592186354503 :question/id #uuid "cb4b46a5-3d8b-55b1-a7c8-ab6f3feb209f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86717"} {:db/id 17592186373237 :question/id #uuid "ba7d8609-104d-5a2e-b355-39ac5dc83584" :question/math-academy-id "q-79838"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"} {:db/id 17592186124377 :question/id #uuid "febc72d4-b1b8-52c9-a805-013b30695077" :question/math-academy-id "e-6876" :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/problem "Estimate $\sin (3) + \cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \sin x + \cos y$ at the point $\left( \pi, 0 \right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =\operatorname{cos}⁡x\,\text{d}x-\operatorname{sin}⁡y\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $\left( \pi, 0 \right),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =\operatorname{cos}⁡\pi \,\text{d}x-\operatorname{sin}⁡0\,\text{d}y \\  & =-1\,\text{d}x+0\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(\pi + \Delta x, 0 + \Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
\Delta f \approx  -1  \,  \Delta x + 0  \,  \Delta y
$$

Note that 

$$
\sin (3) + \cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(3,0.2).$

Moving from $(\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =3-\pi ≈-0.141, \\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈-1(-0.141)+0(0.2) \\  & ≈0.141.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(3,0.2) & ≈f(\pi ,0)+0.141 \\ \operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.2) & ≈\operatorname{sin}⁡(\pi )+\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0.141 \\  & ≈0+1+0.141 \\  & ≈1.141.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186125051 :question/id #uuid "c4f3d8c4-d372-512f-a12b-325fd0cb0744" :question/math-academy-id "q-37281" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b48ae87-4176-58e0-812f-d85f6773ef32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.215" :db/id 17592186321605} {:answer/id #uuid "3d19300c-6ea3-57b7-95c7-ec16fb63521e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.873" :db/id 17592186321622} {:answer/id #uuid "499e8720-d131-55b2-ba8b-eed19cb2b0cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.235" :db/id 17592186321725} {:answer/id #uuid "90606f37-bff4-5fe0-962f-40b81e027f91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.556" :db/id 17592186322292} {:answer/id #uuid "b89c9f1a-e6f4-5b65-b8c8-c7617859f22f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.417" :db/id 17592186322581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89c9f1a-e6f4-5b65-b8c8-c7617859f22f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.417" :db/id 17592186322581} :answer-field/id #uuid "323fec80-bc80-56a8-ad29-27e2aee73e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Estimate $\sqrt[3]{10}+\sqrt{150}$ using a differential $\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\sqrt[3]{x}+\sqrt{y}$ at the point $(8,144).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =\frac{1}{3{x}^{2/3}}\,\text{d}x+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(8,144),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =\frac{1}{3\cdot {8}^{2/3}}\,\text{d}x+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{144}}\,\text{d}y \\  & =\frac{1}{12}\,\text{d}x+\frac{1}{24}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(8+Δx,144+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈\frac{1}{12}\,Δx+\frac{1}{24}\,Δy
$$

Note that 

$$
\sqrt[3]{10}+\sqrt{150}=f(10,150),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(10,150).$

Moving from $(8,144)$ to $(10,150),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-8=2, \\ Δy & =150-144=6.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\frac{1}{12}(2)+\frac{1}{24}(6) \\  & ≈\frac{1}{6}+\frac{1}{4} \\  & ≈\frac{5}{12} \\  & ≈0.417.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.417.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(10,150) & ≈f(8,144)+0.417 \\ \sqrt[3]{10}+\sqrt{150} & ≈\sqrt[3]{8}+\sqrt{144}+0.417 \\  & ≈2+12+0.417 \\  & ≈14.417.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186125227 :question/id #uuid "d809b121-2117-5794-bb89-efcea5057754" :question/math-academy-id "q-79837" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0516f7dd-9d93-57c6-ac01-af2cb388af77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.15" :db/id 17592186345393} {:answer/id #uuid "2c46633a-f843-53a2-9bce-50673065b712" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.19" :db/id 17592186345747} {:answer/id #uuid "68f6bd37-5d74-5a8a-a983-e540fc0d5f12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.16" :db/id 17592186346261} {:answer/id #uuid "77700bc0-d51f-5b58-93c4-32ba10d18ec7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.18" :db/id 17592186346400} {:answer/id #uuid "c5edcb38-7406-54f1-8a3a-1da16bc7f752" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.20" :db/id 17592186347073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77700bc0-d51f-5b58-93c4-32ba10d18ec7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.18" :db/id 17592186346400} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67277c2-ffd2-5937-8d1e-923e1b9e37f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Estimate $(1.81)\cdot \operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)$ using a differential $\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =\operatorname{ln}⁡y\,\text{d}x+\frac{x}{y}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(2,1),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\,\text{d}x+\frac{2}{1}\,\text{d}y \\  & =0\,\text{d}x+2\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈0\,Δx+2\,Δy
$$

Note that 

$$
(1.81)\cdot \operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$

Moving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\ Δy & =0.91-1=-0.09.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\cdot (-0.19)+2\cdot (-0.09) \\  & ≈0-0.18 \\  & ≈-0.18.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & ≈f(2,1)-0.18 \\ (1.81)\cdot \operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91) & ≈2\operatorname{ln}⁡1-0.18 \\  & ≈0-0.18 \\  & ≈-0.18.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186284580 :question/id #uuid "862d8f4a-ef1a-5220-a521-4d8cdae9c576" :question/math-academy-id "q-167170"} {:db/id 17592186284581 :question/id #uuid "9eb149a3-bdcc-552e-a682-ced670cd4013" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171"} {:db/id 17592186348300 :question/id #uuid "2e921641-2f51-5af1-85b0-a27e011ac9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79836"} {:db/id 17592186348301 :question/id #uuid "3c9a3a87-a14e-5269-82cd-f0a320dbf13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79839"} {:db/id 17592186348302 :question/id #uuid "54765bb6-f6e1-50b8-8581-fc92ecfe7a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-79840"} {:db/id 17592186348303 :question/id #uuid "6467df78-2b9b-5255-9ede-8186a25e3f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79841"} {:db/id 17592186348304 :question/id #uuid "1639694c-cbca-54ad-bf88-2314edcecfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79842" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5677a6cf-3ae1-5c05-a7ea-d18c541cd27a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id 17592186346086} {:answer/id #uuid "924a5a3d-80d3-5178-be63-b38474407f0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id 17592186346630} {:answer/id #uuid "c0638f2c-fe79-54bf-9fa1-4ff52d6c1be9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id 17592186347015} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfbad30-45da-5493-a15a-e1bb6fe1f0a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id 17592186347138} {:answer/id #uuid "dc032e4b-974c-59fc-a8c3-0ad4f14b6c8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id 17592186347279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc032e4b-974c-59fc-a8c3-0ad4f14b6c8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id 17592186347279} :answer-field/id #uuid "c553bfad-eb56-5870-b224-d71594c0704b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Estimate $2.1{e}^{0.2}$ using a differential $\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & ={e}^{y}\,\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(2,0),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & ={e}^{0}\,\text{d}x+2\cdot {e}^{0}\,\text{d}y \\  & =1\,\text{d}x+2\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈1\,Δx+2\,Δy
$$

Note that 

$$
2.1{e}^{0.2}=f(2.1,0.2),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(2.1,0.2).$

Moving from $(2,0)$ to $(2.1,0.2),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =2.1-2=0.1, \\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\cdot (0.1)+2\cdot (0.2) \\  & ≈0.1+0.4 \\  & ≈0.5.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.5.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(2.1,0.2) & ≈f(2,0)+0.5 \\ 2.1{e}^{0.2} & ≈2{e}^{0}+0.5 \\  & ≈2+0.5 \\  & ≈2.5.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186348306 :question/id #uuid "6749d91f-49a8-5f4a-a9d1-89ed10459b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79876"} {:db/id 17592186354503 :question/id #uuid "cb4b46a5-3d8b-55b1-a7c8-ab6f3feb209f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86717" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055e28da-53a3-56a0-936c-0d9671fd3eee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id 17592186351615} {:answer/id #uuid "2002afe9-57a6-5eea-89b1-769b51a8d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6" :db/id 17592186351850} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3528b6-af86-5cee-8196-f85faa9431c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id 17592186352631} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d1f5fe-3a65-577e-bd22-9c9dffc1c379" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id 17592186353096} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e7de6-cb92-58e2-8129-ce019b963249" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.6" :db/id 17592186353712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a3528b6-af86-5cee-8196-f85faa9431c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id 17592186352631} :answer-field/id #uuid "de653567-a501-57e8-a380-cad8d0781232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/problem "Estimate ${e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}$ using a differential $\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =y{e}^{xy}\,\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(0,2),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =2\cdot {e}^{0\cdot 2}\,\text{d}x+0\cdot {e}^{0\cdot 2}\,\text{d}y \\  & =2\,\text{d}x+0\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,2+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈2\,Δx+0\,Δy.
$$

Note that 

$$
{e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}=f(0.3,2.15),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(0.3,2.15).$

Moving from $(0,2)$ to $(0.3,2.15),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.3-0=0.3, \\ Δy & =2.15-2=0.15.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈2\cdot (0.3)+0\cdot (0.15) \\  & ≈0.6\,.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.6.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(0.3,2.15) & ≈f(0,2)+0.6 \\ {e}^{(0.3)(2.15)} & ≈{e}^{(0)(2)}+0.6 \\  & ≈1+0.6 \\  & ≈1.6\,.\end{aligned}
$$"} {:db/id 17592186373237 :question/id #uuid "ba7d8609-104d-5a2e-b355-39ac5dc83584" :question/math-academy-id "q-79838" :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5523408e-53c1-55c6-9d6c-93f6067c5024" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.138" :db/id 17592186373239} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0e8bda-987d-556d-adc4-5475e67e3649" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.146" :db/id 17592186373240} {:answer/id #uuid "85457950-05dd-515a-979c-3c810b65e48e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.142" :db/id 17592186373241} {:answer/id #uuid "0e12f9b2-e25d-5e0c-8da0-288cbfb87a9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.144" :db/id 17592186373242} {:answer/id #uuid "64ebbe04-7bf2-5612-a63d-daf300abef15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.140" :db/id 17592186373243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85457950-05dd-515a-979c-3c810b65e48e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.142" :db/id 17592186373241} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0095e22-2029-58c2-ab81-2e1546884b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/problem "Estimate $\sqrt{3.9}\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5)$ using a differential $\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\sqrt{x}\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ at the point $(4,\frac{\pi }{2}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y \\  & =\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2\sqrt{x}}\,\text{d}x-\sqrt{x}\operatorname{sin}⁡y\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

At the point $(4,\frac{\pi }{2}),$ the differential is

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{\pi }{2})}{2\sqrt{4}}\,\text{d}x-\sqrt{4}\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{2})\,\text{d}y \\  & =0\,\text{d}x-2\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

So, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,\frac{\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as

$$
Δf≈0\,Δx-2\,Δy
$$

Note that 

$$
\sqrt{3.9}\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5)=f(3.9,1.5),
$$

so we want to move to the point $(3.9,1.5).$

Moving from $(4,\frac{\pi }{2})$ to $(3.9,1.5),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δx & =3.9-4=-0.1, \\ Δy & =1.5-\frac{\pi }{2}≈-0.070796.\end{aligned}
$$

So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\cdot (-0.1)-2(-0.070796) \\  & ≈0+0.141592 \\  & ≈0.141592.\end{aligned}
$$

This means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141592.$ Consequently, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}f(3.9,1.5) & ≈f(4,\frac{\pi }{2})+0.141592 \\ \sqrt{3.9}\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.5) & ≈\sqrt{4}\operatorname{cos}⁡(\frac{\pi }{2})+0.141592 \\  & ≈0+0.141592 \\  & ≈0.142,\end{aligned}
$$

rounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id 17592186049438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6165f20f-a911-5f52-b154-c9b7146be783" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186282518} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c165-90a1-52b3-950c-0524d0a78d44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186282758} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186283452} {:answer/id #uuid "d1675874-99c7-53bb-a75e-8153ff7cfa34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186283517} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5c20cb-3aaf-53d0-987f-6298d27bfd52" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186283756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186283452} :answer-field/id #uuid "8707a5fd-f08d-52ad-bc81-12c73efa5a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284168} {:answer/id #uuid "6165f20f-a911-5f52-b154-c9b7146be783" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186282518} {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c165-90a1-52b3-950c-0524d0a78d44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186282758} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc9fa5-06d8-5791-9f28-2f5da3679ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186283452} {:answer/id #uuid "d1675874-99c7-53bb-a75e-8153ff7cfa34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186283517} {:answer/id #uuid "ec