 :step/id #uuid "bf1f5b6c-b40c-5ff9-9ed6-50ee5d23ff70" :step/math-academy-id 25383 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125478}} {:db/id 17592186125478 :step/content {:db/id 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124375 :question/id #uuid "bb3ab0dc-7e26-58f5-a0c2-564c01c77f2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6854"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186125053} {:db/id 17592186125057} {:db/id 17592186284605} {:db/id 17592186348270} {:db/id 17592186348271} {:db/id 17592186348321} {:db/id 17592186372349} {:db/id 17592186375377} {:db/id 17592186375384} {:db/id 17592186393968}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"} :step/id #uuid "34d39ab5-5a0c-5548-9b37-4b9ff25664cc" :step/math-academy-id 25385 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125480}} {:db/id 17592186125479 :step/content {:db/id 17592186125813 :tutorial/content "The **differential** of a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ is denoted $\textrm df(x,y)$ and represents the infinitesimal change in the function value that occurs when the inputs $x$ and $y$ are changed by infinitesimal amounts $\textrm dx$ and $\textrm dy.$

The formula for the differential comes from the multivariable chain rule, which can be expressed as

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y.\end{aligned}
$$

Note that the differential can be extended to any number of variables. For example, for a three-variable function $f(x,y,z),$ we have

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y+(\frac{∂f}{∂z})\,\text{d}z.\end{aligned}
$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "549aa462-1d86-5880-b6e2-2f6b1d79b41d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4191 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ca5fbd6f-0bec-58b1-a606-32a0d7df8952" :step/math-academy-id 25416 :step/next {:db/id 17592186125477}} {:db/id 17592186125480 :step/content {:db/id 17592186125814 :tutorial/content "Given a multivariable function $f(x,y),$ the differential

$$
\begin{aligned}\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,\text{d}x+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,\text{d}y\end{aligned}
$$

tells us how infinitesimal changes in $x$ and $y,$ denoted $\textrm dx$ and $\textrm dy,$ result in an infinitesimal change in $f,$ denoted $\textrm df.$

Consequently, we can use the differential to *approximate* the change in $f$ as we move from one point to another. If we move from a point $(x,y)$ to a point $(x + \Delta x, y + \Delta y),$ then the approximate change in $f$ is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}Δf(x,y) & \approx (\frac{∂f}{∂x})\,Δx+(\frac{∂f}{∂y})\,Δy.\end{aligned}
$$

